Author:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
AuthorDate: Mon Oct 12 11:57:28 2026 -0500
Commit:     totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
CommitDate: Mon Oct 12 11:57:28 2026 -0500

    fix(export): refuse non-bare target_repo with 400 invalid_target_repo
    
    A target_repo pointing at a local non-bare working repository whose
    checked-out branch is the push branch can never accept the push — git
    rejects it with "refusing to update checked out branch" — but the export
    reached git push and leaked that raw failure as HTTP 500 export_failed.
    
    The engine now validates the push target before any clone, write, or
    commit: when Push is set and RepoURL resolves to a local non-bare repo
    (plain path or file:// URL) whose checked-out branch is the push branch
    and receive.denyCurrentBranch is not ignore/warn/updateInstead, Export
    returns the typed ErrNonBareTarget naming target_repo. The handler maps
    it to 400 invalid_target_repo. Remote URLs, missing paths, bare repos,
    detached HEADs, pushes to a non-checked-out branch, and dry runs are
    untouched; bare/remote export success behavior is preserved.
    
    Regression coverage: engine tests for the refusal (including
    before-mutation ordering and the dry-run exemption), the allowed
    non-checked-out-branch push, and the local-vs-remote path
    classification; a handler test asserting the 400 invalid_target_repo
    contract against a real non-bare target. RED-proven: with the
    production change reverted, the handler test fails with the exact
    dogfood-evidence response (500 export_failed carrying git's "refusing
    to update checked out branch").
    
    docs/api-reference.md and pkg/api/openapi.yaml now state that
    target_repo must be a bare repository or a remote that accepts pushes,
    with the 400 invalid_target_repo shape. The spec edit was verified
    through pkg/api's own YAML parser (all 19 schemas intact).
    
    Addresses DF-OFF-BY-ONE-35.
    
    Co-authored-by: Alexis Okuwa <wojonstech@gmail.com>
